The actual model parameter being fired in the request **does include the `:free` suffix**.

I found this in your server logs (terminal output), which are generated by the `console.log` in `src/lib/evaluator.ts`. Here is exactly what is being sent to OpenRouter:

### Outgoing Request JSON (Example)
```json
[EVALUATOR] Request for nousresearch/hermes-3-llama-3.1-405b:free: {
  "model": "nousresearch/hermes-3-llama-3.1-405b:free",
  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "system",
      "content": "You are a helpful assistant..."
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "how much wood could a woodchuck chuck..."
    }
  ],
  "temperature": 0.7,
  "stream": true,
  "stream_options": { "include_usage": true }
}
```

### The Code Responsible
In `src/lib/evaluator.ts`, the `modelId` (which contains the `:free` suffix) is passed directly into the request body:

```typescript:56:62:src/lib/evaluator.ts
  const body: any = {
    model: modelId,
    messages: fullMessages,
    temperature,
    stream: true,
    stream_options: { include_usage: true },
  };
```

And then logged right before the `fetch` call:

```typescript:97:97:src/lib/evaluator.ts
  console.log(`[EVALUATOR] Request for ${modelId}:`, JSON.stringify(body, null, 2));
```

So yes, the `:free` tag is definitely in the outgoing requests. This ensures Open
Router routes the request to the free tier of those versions of the models.

